---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import RefEntries from '../../components/RefEntries.astro';
import { sklearnGroups, sklearnEntries } from '../../data/sklearn';
---

<Base title="scikit-learn reference" description="Every scikit-learn class and function the notes use, in plain words, with a runnable example and its real output.">
  <div class="note-layout">
    <article class="prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Reference · {sklearnEntries.length} entries</p>
      <h1>scikit-learn, as the notes use it</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Every scikit-learn name that appears in a code block on this site has an entry here, and clicking the name in
        the code brings you to it. Each entry says what it does in plain words, which note teaches the idea behind it,
        and shows a small example with its real output.
      </p>
      <Callout kind="plain" label="The three ideas that explain all of scikit-learn">
        <p>
          <strong>1. Everything is an object you make, then fit.</strong> <code>model = LinearRegression()</code> makes
          an untrained model. <code>model.fit(X, y)</code> trains it. Preprocessing steps work the same way.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>2. Models predict, preprocessing steps transform.</strong> A model has <code>predict</code>; a scaler
          or encoder has <code>transform</code>. Both learn what they need in <code>fit</code>, from training data only.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>3. X is a table, y is a column.</strong> X is 2-D: one row per example, one column per feature. y is
          1-D: one answer per row. Almost every error message for beginners is about this shape.
        </p>
      </Callout>
      <RefEntries groups={sklearnGroups} entries={sklearnEntries} />
    </article>
    <aside class="note-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <strong>On this page</strong>
      {sklearnGroups.map((g) => <a href={`#g-${g.id}`}>{g.title}</a>)}
    </aside>
  </div>
</Base>
